import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Stdlib migration to Handl — file requirements, phases, inventory","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Stdlib migration to Handl — file requirements, phases, inventory","type":"rfc","date":"2026-09-03T00:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","refs":["ADR-0077"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/rfcs/0077-stdlib-migration.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/rfcs/0077-stdlib-migration.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/rfcs/0077-stdlib-migration.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="stdlib-migration-to-handl-—-file-requirements-phases-inventory" tabindex="-1">Stdlib migration to Handl — file requirements, phases, inventory <a class="header-anchor" href="#stdlib-migration-to-handl-—-file-requirements-phases-inventory" aria-label="Permalink to “Stdlib migration to Handl — file requirements, phases, inventory”">​</a></h1><p>Port plan moved verbatim from ADR-0077 (ADR-0161 size budget). The decision and the Rust boundary live in the ADR; the phase detail, per-file requirements and the per-library inventory live here.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><h3 id="the-current-state" tabindex="-1">The current state <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-current-state" aria-label="Permalink to “The current state”">​</a></h3><p>The <code>plugin-lib-*</code> family of crates (~60 crates) implements the handl standard library. Each crate is Rust. They fall into two populations:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Pure-logic libs</strong> (<code>hello</code>, <code>stats</code>, <code>collections</code>, <code>utils</code>, <code>parse</code>, …) — the logic is already handl-authored in <code>.hnl</code> files under <code>handl-packages/</code>, but each crate carries ~400 lines of hand-rolled Rust bridge code (<code>bridge.rs</code>, <code>module.rs</code>, <code>plugin.rs</code>, <code>lib.rs</code>) that <code>include_str!</code>s the <code>.hnl</code> source, builds a cached per-thread <code>Runtime</code>, and evaluates <code>(source).func(args)</code> per call.</p></li><li><p><strong>Native-primitive libs</strong> (<code>base64</code>, <code>crypto</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>http</code>, <code>tls</code>, <code>os</code>, <code>rand</code>, …) — the logic <em>and</em> the implementation are Rust, because they wrap Rust crates or syscalls. There is no handl source for them.</p></li></ol><h3 id="what-already-exists" tabindex="-1">What already exists <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-already-exists" aria-label="Permalink to “What already exists”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Eval-side module registry</strong> (<code>plugin-module</code>): <code>Module</code> trait, <code>ModuleRegistry</code>, <code>ModuleLookup</code>, <code>ModuleRule</code>/<code>ModuleRuntime</code>. The tree-walk eval engine dispatches <code>ArrSource::Foreign(name)</code> to <code>ModuleLookup::lookup(name)</code> → Rust <code>Function::call(args)</code>.</li><li><strong>Inference-side module loader</strong> (<code>plugin-ext-refinement</code>): <code>ModuleLoader</code> (ADR-0069) loads <code>.hnl</code> files, parses <code>import</code>/<code>export</code>, produces <code>HashMap&lt;String, Scheme&gt;</code> (named arrow graphs) + cached contracts. All three normalization strategies (A: compile-time desugar, B: flatten, C: runtime lazy load) are implemented. This feeds refinement inference only — <strong>not</strong> eval.</li><li><strong>Axiom system</strong> (<code>plugin-ext-refinement</code>): <code>axiom name : T -&gt; T, guarantee: P</code> declarations in <code>.hnl</code> contract files. Loaded into <code>ContractRegistry</code>. Used for static refinement inference. <strong>Not</strong> wired into the eval-time foreign-call boundary.</li><li><strong><code>ArrSource::Foreign(name)</code></strong> in <code>Scheme</code> IR — the capability-tracked foreign-call marker. The tree-walk engine dispatches it to <code>ModuleLookup</code>. But the foreign name, its type, and its contract all live in Rust, not in handl source.</li></ul><h3 id="the-gap" tabindex="-1">The gap <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-gap" aria-label="Permalink to “The gap”">​</a></h3><p>The two module systems (eval-side registry, inference-side loader) are not connected. There is no path from a <code>.hnl</code> file to a callable eval-time <code>Module</code> without a per-library Rust bridge. And for native libs, there is no handl surface syntax for declaring foreign bindings — the foreign name, type, and contract are all Rust-authored.</p><p>A partial bridge (<code>plugin-lib-handl</code> with <code>HandlModule</code> / <code>HandlLibraryPlugin</code>) was just built and proven by migrating <code>plugin-lib-stats</code>. It eliminates the per-library bridge for pure-logic libs. But it does not address native libs, axioms, or capability declaration.</p><h3 id="why-axioms-and-proofs-must-live-in-handl" tabindex="-1">Why axioms and proofs must live in handl <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-axioms-and-proofs-must-live-in-handl" aria-label="Permalink to “Why axioms and proofs must live in handl”">​</a></h3><p>Rust offers no proof mechanism. The stdlib&#39;s correctness story — refinement contracts, termination guarantees, compositional inference — can only be expressed and verified in handl. Today the axioms for irreducible Rust primitives live in <code>handl-packages/contracts/prim.hnl</code>, but they are disconnected from the actual Rust <code>Function</code> impls they describe, and they are not enforced at the eval boundary.</p><p>For the migration to be complete, every public arrow in the stdlib must carry:</p><ul><li>A <code>---</code> doc comment (the public API contract in prose).</li><li>An <code>axiom</code> declaration (the formal contract: input type, output type, <code>guarantee</code>, optional <code>requires</code>/<code>termination</code>).</li><li>A proof (where the body is handl-authored — the proof is compositional via the contract cache, ADR-0069 §4).</li></ul><p>For native libs, the Rust body cannot be proven. But the <em>axiom</em> can be stated in handl, and the Rust body is trusted to honor it. This is the FFI boundary: handl declares and verifies the contract; Rust implements it.</p><h3 id="requirement-pure-handl-result-library-for-error-handling" tabindex="-1">Requirement: pure-handl <code>result</code> library for error handling <a class="header-anchor" href="#requirement-pure-handl-result-library-for-error-handling" aria-label="Permalink to “Requirement: pure-handl result library for error handling”">​</a></h3><p><strong>This is a hard requirement, not a nice-to-have.</strong> The stdlib cannot be written in handl without a pure-handl <code>result</code> type for graceful error handling. Today the <code>result</code> pattern (<code>result.ok</code>, <code>result.err</code>, <code>result.is_ok</code>, <code>result.is_err</code>) is implemented in <strong>Rust</strong> — <code>ResultModule</code> in <code>plugin-lib-utils/src/result/module.rs</code> constructs <code>{&quot;_tag&quot;: &quot;Ok&quot;, &quot;value&quot;: &lt;T&gt;}</code> / <code>{&quot;_tag&quot;: &quot;Err&quot;, &quot;error&quot;: &lt;E&gt;}</code> JSON objects via Rust <code>Function</code> impls. The <code>.hnl</code> file (<code>handl-packages/utils/utils.hnl</code>) carries doc comments and <code>cap</code> declarations for these, but the implementation is Rust.</p><p>This blocks the migration: every handl-authored stdlib function that can fail (parse, lookup, conversion, FFI calls) needs <code>result</code> to return errors structurally instead of crashing. If <code>result</code> stays Rust, then handl stdlib code depends on a Rust module for error handling — defeating the point of the migration and creating a circular dependency (handl stdlib -&gt; Rust result module -&gt; handl stdlib for its own logic).</p><p><strong>The <code>result</code> library must be migrated to pure handl as part of Phase 1, before any other stdlib function that uses error handling is migrated.</strong></p><h3 id="requirement-language-level-tag-declarations-prerequisite-for-result" tabindex="-1">Requirement: language-level tag declarations (prerequisite for <code>result</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#requirement-language-level-tag-declarations-prerequisite-for-result" aria-label="Permalink to “Requirement: language-level tag declarations (prerequisite for result)”">​</a></h3><p>The pure-handl <code>result</code> library requires a <strong>language-level tag system</strong> -- a nominal identity registry so that <code>Ok</code> and <code>Err</code> are first-class tags with structural identity, not hand-built <code>{&quot;_tag&quot;: &quot;Ok&quot;, ...}</code> JSON objects that rely on string-matching conventions.</p><p><strong>What already exists -- <code>plugin-lib-tag</code>:</strong></p><p>The tag system already exists as a host-exposed library (<code>crates/plugin-lib-tag/</code>, ADR-0055). It exposes seven arrows declared in <code>handl-packages/tag/tag.hnl</code> via the <code>op</code>/<code>handl</code> form:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Arrow</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>tag.tag(name, payload)</code></td><td><strong>mint</strong> a tagged value <code>{&quot;__tag__&quot;: name, ...payload}</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>tag.untag(v)</code></td><td>return the payload of a tagged value</td></tr><tr><td><code>tag.tag_of(v)</code></td><td>return the tag name string, or null</td></tr><tr><td><code>tag.is_tag(v)</code></td><td>test whether <code>v</code> is a tagged value</td></tr><tr><td><code>tag.construct(name, payload)</code></td><td>mint a registry-scoped type value (ADR-0054)</td></tr><tr><td><code>tag.match_type(v, cases)</code></td><td>dispatch on the type tag (ADR-0054)</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>tag.match_tag</code> is <strong>not needed</strong> -- the language already has a <code>match</code> construct that dispatches on tag identity. The tag sugar&#39;s <code>match r { ok(v) =&gt; ... }</code> desugars to the native <code>match</code>, not to a library arrow. <code>tag.match_tag</code> can be removed from <code>plugin-lib-tag</code>.</p><p>The <code>__tag__</code> key is <strong>reserved</strong> -- user object literals cannot produce it (the eval engines reject it), so the only way a value acquires a <code>__tag__</code> key is through <code>tag.tag</code> (forgery prevention, ADR-0055 S3). This is already a nominal identity system: <code>tag.tag(&quot;ok&quot;, {value: 5})</code> and <code>tag.tag(&quot;err&quot;, {value: 5})</code> are distinguishable by tag name, not by string-matching a user field.</p><p><strong>What is missing -- surface syntax sugar:</strong></p><p>The tag library arrows exist, but there is no surface syntax to declare and use them ergonomically. Today you&#39;d need to manually compose arrows that construct <code>tag.tag</code> payloads -- verbose and error-prone (tag names are string literals, not declared entities). The <code>tag</code> keyword is <strong>sugar</strong> that generates the constructor arrows at the Scheme level -- no new primitive, no engine change, no surface-syntax <code>tag.tag(...)</code> calls.</p><p><strong>What the sugar looks like:</strong></p><p>Declaring tags:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>tag ok = Value</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>tag err = Error</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This desugars at the <strong>Scheme level</strong>, not in surface syntax. The desugar pass registers <code>ok</code> and <code>err</code> in the tag registry (a data-driven <code>Language</code> entry, per ADR-0044) and generates constructor arrows directly as arrow-calculus <code>Scheme</code> -- composing <code>arr</code> (lift the payload construction) with <code>&amp;&amp;&amp;</code> (fanout: tag name constant + payload) and <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> (flow into the <code>tag.tag</code> host arrow). No surface-syntax call to <code>tag.tag(...)</code> -- that would be function application, not arrow composition.</p><p>The declaration also registers the tag name + payload type in the <code>Language</code> tag registry so the typechecker can enforce tag identity nominally (an <code>ok</code> value is not an <code>err</code> value, even if the payload type is the same).</p><p>Constructing a tagged value -- compose via <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> (arrow flow):</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>42 &gt;&gt;&gt; ok        -- ok is an arrow: Value -&gt; Tagged ok Value</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>&quot;nope&quot; &gt;&gt;&gt; err   -- err is an arrow: Error -&gt; Tagged err Error</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>ok</code> and <code>err</code> are arrows. You compose them in a pipeline, you don&#39;t apply them like functions. This is arrow calculus, not lambda calculus. The <code>tag.tag</code> host arrow is wired in at the Scheme level during desugar -- the user never writes <code>tag.tag(...)</code> call syntax.</p><p>Checking tag identity (sugar for <code>tag.tag_of</code> + compare):</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>r is ok(_)    -- desugars to: tag.tag_of(r) == &quot;ok&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>r is err(_)   -- desugars to: tag.tag_of(r) == &quot;err&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Pattern matching uses the language&#39;s native <code>match</code> -- no library arrow needed:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>match r {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ok(v)  =&gt; on_ok(v),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  err(e) =&gt; on_err(e),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The native <code>match</code> dispatches on tag identity and binds the payload fields by name, so <code>ok(v)</code> binds <code>v</code> to the <code>value</code> field of the <code>ok</code>-tagged payload. No <code>tag.match_tag</code> library arrow required.</p><p><strong>Relationship to ADR-0082 (<code>data</code>):</strong></p><p><code>data</code> declarations (ADR-0082) generate predicates, constructors, and recursors for inductive types. The constructors today use <code>{ tag: &quot;Zero&quot; }</code> string-keyed JSON objects -- unregistered, no nominal identity, forgeable by user code. With the tag sugar, <code>data</code> constructors would desugar to <code>tag.tag</code> calls instead:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>data Nat = Zero | Succ(Nat)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Would generate constructors like:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>tag zero = Unit</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>tag succ = Nat</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>-- Constructors are arrows, composed via &gt;&gt;&gt;:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>-- () &gt;&gt;&gt; zero   -- zero is an arrow: Unit -&gt; Tagged zero Unit</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>-- n &gt;&gt;&gt; succ    -- succ is an arrow: Nat -&gt; Tagged succ Nat</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This gives <code>data</code> constructors the same nominal identity + forgery prevention as user-declared tags. The <code>data</code> desugar (ADR-0082) would emit <code>tag</code> declarations instead of string-keyed object literals. However, <code>data</code> is a larger feature (recursors, predicates, strict positivity); the tag sugar is the simpler prerequisite that both <code>result</code> and <code>data</code> build on.</p><p><strong>Design notes:</strong></p><ul><li>The <code>tag</code> keyword is <strong>pure sugar</strong> -- it desugars to calls to <code>tag.tag</code> (construction) and <code>tag.tag_of</code> (inspection). Pattern matching uses the language&#39;s native <code>match</code>, not a library arrow. No new native op, no engine change, no new <code>TaggedValue</code> variant. The host capability (<code>plugin-lib-tag</code>) already does the work.</li><li>Tags are a <strong>nominal</strong> type system feature: identity is by tag name, not by structural shape. <code>ok(5)</code> and <code>err(5)</code> have the same payload but different tag identity. The <code>__tag__</code> key is reserved so user code can&#39;t forge tagged values.</li><li>Tag declarations are <strong>data-driven</strong>: a <code>tag</code> declaration adds an entry to the <code>Language</code> tag registry (tag name + payload type). No engine recompile. This is consistent with ADR-0044 (&quot;language as data&quot;) and the engine-genericity rule.</li><li>Tag declarations should be <strong>exportable</strong> (via the <code>export</code> mechanism, Phase 6) so that <code>result</code>&#39;s <code>ok</code>/<code>err</code> tags are importable by any stdlib module that needs error handling.</li></ul><p><strong>Implementation plan:</strong></p><ol><li>Add <code>tag</code> declaration syntax to the grammar (<code>grammar.yaml</code>) and AST (<code>ast.yaml</code>): <code>tag &lt;name&gt; = &lt;payload_type&gt;</code> as a top-level form.</li><li>Add a tag registry to <code>Language</code> (data-driven): a map from tag name to its payload type. Tags are registered when a <code>.hnl</code> file declaring them is loaded.</li><li>Desugar <code>tag &lt;name&gt; = &lt;type&gt;</code> to a constructor arrow at the Scheme level -- compose <code>arr</code> (payload construction) with <code>&amp;&amp;&amp;</code> (fanout: constant tag name + payload) and <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> (flow into <code>tag.tag</code> host arrow). The constructor is an arrow <code>&lt;name&gt; : Payload -&gt; Tagged</code>, composed via <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>, never called via function-application syntax. Field names match the payload type&#39;s fields if it&#39;s a record.</li><li>Desugar <code>expr is &lt;tag_name&gt;(&lt;var&gt;)</code> to <code>tag.tag_of(expr) == &quot;&lt;tag_name&gt;&quot;</code>.</li><li><code>match expr { &lt;tag&gt;(&lt;var&gt;) =&gt; body, ... }</code> uses the language&#39;s native <code>match</code> -- no desugar needed, just ensure <code>match</code> recognizes tag patterns (dispatch on <code>__tag__</code> identity, bind payload fields).</li><li>Remove <code>tag.match_tag</code> from <code>plugin-lib-tag</code> -- it&#39;s redundant with the native <code>match</code>.</li><li>Migrate <code>result</code> to use <code>tag ok</code> / <code>tag err</code> sugar instead of <code>{&quot;_tag&quot;: &quot;Ok&quot;, ...}</code> JSON objects.</li><li>Migrate <code>data</code> constructors (ADR-0082) to desugar to <code>tag</code> declarations (generating constructor arrows at the Scheme level) instead of <code>{ tag: &quot;...&quot; }</code> string-keyed objects.</li></ol><p><strong>Done when:</strong> <code>tag ok = Value</code> / <code>tag err = Error</code> can be declared in <code>.hnl</code> source; <code>v &gt;&gt;&gt; ok</code> / <code>e &gt;&gt;&gt; err</code> compose with the constructor arrows (which wire into <code>tag.tag</code> at the Scheme level); <code>r is ok(_)</code> checks via <code>tag.tag_of</code>; <code>match r { ok(v) =&gt; ..., err(e) =&gt; ... }</code> dispatches via the native <code>match</code>; <code>tag.match_tag</code> is removed from <code>plugin-lib-tag</code>; the typechecker recognizes tag identity; <code>result</code> is implemented using the tag sugar, not JSON objects. This is a prerequisite for the pure-handl <code>result</code> library, which is itself a prerequisite for Phase 1.</p><p><strong>The pure-handl <code>result</code> implementation using tag sugar:</strong></p><p>Only the constructors need to migrate. Predicates (<code>is_ok</code>, <code>is_err</code>), extraction (<code>unwrap_or</code>), and dispatch (<code>match</code>) are already covered by <code>mood</code> (the core interrogative feature) and native <code>match</code> -- no wrappers needed.</p><p><code>mood</code> is the core language feature for interrogatives:</p><ul><li><code>mood.int(r, ok)</code> -- is r an ok-tagged value? (replaces <code>result.is_ok</code>)</li><li><code>mood.int(r, err)</code> -- is r an err-tagged value? (replaces <code>result.is_err</code>)</li><li><code>mood.norm(r, ok)</code> -- project ok payload or return err result</li><li><code>mood.ind(r, ok)</code> -- project ok payload or error</li><li><code>match r { ok(v) =&gt; ..., err(e) =&gt; ... }</code> -- native language match (replaces <code>result.match</code>)</li></ul><p>Combinators like <code>map</code> and <code>and_then</code> are pure handl stdlib functions built on <code>mood</code> + native <code>match</code> -- they don&#39;t need to be part of this migration.</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>-- Declare the tags (desugars to tag.tag constructor arrows).</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>-- Exported so importers get the tag identity too.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>tag ok = Value</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>tag err = Error</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>--- result.ok -- construct an Ok result wrapping a value.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>--- Contract: IsOk(output)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>result.ok = value ~&gt; value &gt;&gt;&gt; ok</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>--- result.err -- construct an Err result wrapping an error.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>--- Contract: IsErr(output)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>result.err = error ~&gt; error &gt;&gt;&gt; err</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>ok</code> and <code>err</code> are arrows (<code>Value -&gt; Tagged</code>). <code>result.ok</code> composes the input through <code>ok</code> via <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> (arrow flow). No function application.</p><p>That&#39;s it -- two one-liner arrows. The Rust <code>ResultModule</code> (<code>plugin-lib-utils/src/result/module.rs</code>) is deleted; its <code>ok</code>/<code>err</code><code>Function</code> impls are replaced by the two arrows above. Its <code>is_ok</code>/<code>is_err</code> impls are deleted entirely -- use <code>mood.int(r, ok)</code> / <code>mood.int(r, err)</code> directly. The combinator functions, if they exist in Rust today, are already or will be pure handl using <code>mood</code> and native <code>match</code>.</p><p>Each of the two arrows composes with the <code>ok</code>/<code>err</code> constructor arrows (generated at the Scheme level from the <code>tag</code> declarations, which wire in the <code>tag.tag</code> host arrow). No surface-syntax <code>tag.tag(...)</code> calls. No new primitive.</p><p><strong>Done when:</strong> <code>result.ok</code>/<code>err</code> are pure handl arrows composing with the <code>ok</code>/<code>err</code> tag constructor arrows via <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>; the Rust <code>ResultModule</code> and its <code>Function</code> impls are deleted; <code>mood.int(r, ok)</code> / <code>mood.int(r, err)</code> cover predicate checks; all tests pass.</p><h2 id="file-requirements" tabindex="-1">File requirements <a class="header-anchor" href="#file-requirements" aria-label="Permalink to “File requirements”">​</a></h2><p>Every public arrow in the stdlib must carry:</p><ol><li><p><strong><code>---</code> doc comment</strong> — the public API contract in prose, immediately above the arrow definition. This is the documentation surface (rendered by the doc generator, shown in LSP hover).</p></li><li><p><strong><code>axiom</code> declaration</strong> — the formal contract: input type, output type, <code>guarantee</code>, optional <code>requires</code>/<code>termination</code>. This is the verification surface (consumed by the inference engine, enforced at the eval boundary after Phase 3).</p></li><li><p><strong>Proof</strong> — for handl-authored arrows, the proof is compositional via the contract cache (ADR-0069 §4): each arrow is inferred once, its contract cached, importers reuse it. No separate proof file is needed — the inference engine generates the proof from the arrow graph + imported contracts. For <code>op</code> arrows (Rust-backed), no proof is generated — the axiom is trusted (the Rust handl is assumed to honor it), but the contract is still enforced at the boundary (Phase 3).</p></li></ol><p>Example of a complete migrated pure-logic arrow:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>--- stats.sum — sum all numeric elements in an array.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>--- Non-numeric elements are silently skipped.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>--- Contract: SumOf(output, input)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>sum = xs ~&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  loop { xs: xs, acc: 0 }(</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    [xs, acc] ~&gt; match xs {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>      []: { out: acc },</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>      [h, ...t]: if type.type_of(h) == &quot;num&quot; then</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        { state: { xs: t, acc: acc + h } }</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>      else</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        { state: { xs: t, acc: acc } }</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    }</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  )</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Example of a complete migrated native <code>op</code>:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>--- base64.encode — encode a string to base64.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>--- Contract: IsBase64(output)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>op &quot;base64.encode&quot; : String -&gt; String</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  guarantee: IsBase64(output)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>handl &quot;base64.encode&quot; = rust_base64_encode</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="phases" tabindex="-1">Phases <a class="header-anchor" href="#phases" aria-label="Permalink to “Phases”">​</a></h2><h3 id="phase-1-—-pure-logic-libs-via-handlmodule-in-progress" tabindex="-1">Phase 1 — Pure-logic libs via <code>HandlModule</code> (in progress) <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-1-—-pure-logic-libs-via-handlmodule-in-progress" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 1 — Pure-logic libs via HandlModule (in progress)”">​</a></h3><p>Migrate all pure-logic libs (<code>hello</code>, <code>collections</code>, <code>utils</code>, <code>parse</code>, …) to <code>.hnl</code> files loaded by the generic <code>plugin-lib-handl</code> crate (<code>HandlModule</code> / <code>HandlLibraryPlugin</code>), following the proven <code>plugin-lib-stats</code> migration.</p><p><strong>Prerequisites:</strong> The language-level tag system (see &quot;Requirement: language-level tag declarations&quot; above) and the pure-handl <code>result</code> library built on it (see &quot;Requirement: pure-handl <code>result</code> library for error handling&quot; above) must land first. Every other stdlib function that can fail needs <code>result</code> for error handling — migrating them before <code>result</code> is pure handl would leave them dependent on the Rust <code>ResultModule</code>, creating a circular dependency. And <code>result</code> itself needs the tag system so that <code>Ok</code>/<code>Err</code> are nominal tags, not string-keyed JSON objects.</p><p>Each <code>.hnl</code> file carries:</p><ul><li><code>---</code> doc comments on every public arrow (the API contract in prose).</li><li>The arrow logic (already handl-authored for hello/stats; port the rest from Rust).</li></ul><p>The Rust crates collapse to: one <code>plugin-lib-handl</code> crate (already built)</p><ul><li>N <code>.hnl</code> files + a kit manifest entry per lib. No per-library Rust.</li></ul><p><strong>Done when:</strong> <code>plugin-lib-hello</code>, <code>plugin-lib-collections</code>, <code>plugin-lib-utils</code>, <code>plugin-lib-parse</code> (and any other pure-logic libs) are migrated; their Rust bridge code is deleted; all tests pass.</p><h3 id="phase-2-—-op-declaration-form-ffi" tabindex="-1">Phase 2 — <code>op</code> declaration form (FFI) <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-2-—-op-declaration-form-ffi" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 2 — op declaration form (FFI)”">​</a></h3><p>Add surface syntax for declaring operations (Rust-backed functions) in <code>.hnl</code> source, so native libs can declare their interface in handl without a Rust <code>Module</code> struct. The declaration is an <strong>operation</strong> in the algebraic-effects sense (Plotkin &amp; Pretnar; Sanada&#39;s arrow calculus with operations and handlers, ADR-0052 §&quot;Arrow Handlers&quot;); the Rust body that implements it is the <strong>handl</strong> (the handler that interprets the operation).</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>-- fs.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>op &quot;fs.read&quot; : Path -&gt; Bytes</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  requires: PathValid(path)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  guarantee: IsUtf8(output)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>op &quot;fs.write&quot; : (Path × Bytes) -&gt; Unit</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  guarantee: True</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The <code>handl</code> block binds a Rust function (the handler) to an <code>op</code>:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>handl &quot;fs.read&quot; = rust_fs_read</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>handl &quot;fs.write&quot; = rust_fs_write</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This requires:</p><ul><li><strong>Lexer/Pratt parser support</strong> for <code>op</code> and <code>handl</code> declarations (new top-level forms, like <code>import</code>/<code>export</code> in ADR-0069). Keywords <code>token.keyword.op</code> and <code>token.keyword.handl</code> are already registered in <code>tokens.yaml</code> and <code>lexer_rules.yaml</code>.</li><li><strong>An <code>OpDecl</code> node</strong> in the parsed module, carrying the name, type signature, and optional axiom (requires/guarantee/termination).</li><li><strong>A <code>HandlDecl</code> node</strong> in the parsed module, carrying the op name and the Rust function identifier to bind.</li><li><strong>A host-side registry</strong> mapping declared <code>op</code> names → Rust <code>Function</code> impls (the handls). The Rust bodies stay; only the declaration moves to handl.</li><li><strong><code>HandlModule</code> integration</strong> — when a <code>.hnl</code> file contains <code>op</code> declarations, the loaded module exposes them as callable functions that dispatch to the Rust registry (same <code>ArrSource::Foreign</code> path, but the name/type/contract now come from handl source, not Rust).</li></ul><p><strong>Done when:</strong> at least one native lib (e.g. <code>plugin-lib-base64</code>) is migrated — its <code>Module</code>/<code>Function</code> trait impls deleted, replaced by an <code>op</code> declaration in <code>.hnl</code> + a Rust <code>Function</code> (the handl) registered under the declared name. All tests pass.</p><h3 id="phase-3-—-wire-axioms-to-eval" tabindex="-1">Phase 3 — Wire axioms to eval <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-3-—-wire-axioms-to-eval" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 3 — Wire axioms to eval”">​</a></h3><p>Attach <code>ContractRegistry</code> contracts to the <code>Foreign</code> dispatch boundary so that declared <code>op</code>s carry enforced <code>guarantee</code>/<code>requires</code>, not just inferred offline.</p><p>This requires:</p><ul><li><strong>Linking <code>OpDecl</code> axioms to <code>ContractRegistry</code></strong> — when a module with <code>op</code> declarations is loaded, its axioms are registered in the contract registry under the op name.</li><li><strong>Eval-time checking</strong> — the tree-walk engine, when dispatching <code>ArrSource::Foreign(name)</code>, consults the contract registry for <code>name</code> and checks the <code>requires</code> predicate against the call arguments (at runtime; static checking is the inference engine&#39;s job).</li><li><strong>Proof obligation</strong> — for handl-authored arrows, the proof is compositional (ADR-0069 §4 contract cache). For <code>op</code> arrows, the axiom is trusted (the Rust body is assumed to honor it); no proof is generated, but the contract is still enforced at the boundary.</li></ul><p><strong>Done when:</strong> an <code>op</code> with a <code>requires</code> predicate rejects a call that violates it; an <code>op</code> with a <code>guarantee</code> is checked against the return value (where feasible). Tests cover both.</p><h3 id="phase-4-—-native-libs-migrated-to-handl-interfaces" tabindex="-1">Phase 4 — Native libs migrated to handl interfaces <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-4-—-native-libs-migrated-to-handl-interfaces" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 4 — Native libs migrated to handl interfaces”">​</a></h3><p><strong>This phase is replaced by <a href="./0083-ffi-native-stdlib.html">ADR-0083 — FFI-Native Stdlib</a>.</strong></p><p>This phase proposed keeping Rust <code>Function</code> bodies as the <code>handl</code> implementations for native libs (<code>handl &quot;fs.read&quot; = rust_fs_read</code>). ADR-0083 replaces this with direct C/POSIX FFI data bindings (<code>handl &quot;libc.open&quot; = host.ffi_bind &quot;libc&quot; &quot;open&quot; ...</code>), eliminating the Rust body layer entirely.</p><p>The <code>op</code>/<code>handl</code> declaration form (Phase 2) and axiom wiring (Phase 3) remain valid. Only the <code>handl</code> binding target changes: <code>host.ffi_bind</code> instead of <code>rust_x</code>. See ADR-0083 for the full design and migration plan.</p><p><strong>Historical text (superseded):</strong></p><p>Migrate all native-primitive libs (<code>base64</code>, <code>crypto</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>http</code>, <code>tls</code>, <code>os</code>, <code>rand</code>, …) to <code>.hnl</code> interface files using the Phase 2 <code>op</code> form + Phase 3 axiom wiring.</p><p>Each native lib was to become:</p><ul><li>A <code>.hnl</code> file with <code>op</code> declarations + <code>---</code> doc comments + axioms.</li><li>A Rust crate that registers <code>Function</code> impls (the handls) under the declared <code>op</code> names (the bodies — I/O, crypto, syscalls — stay Rust).</li><li>No <code>Module</code>/<code>Plugin</code> trait scaffolding in Rust; the <code>HandlModule</code> / <code>HandlLibraryPlugin</code> from Phase 1 handles registration.</li></ul><p>The Rust that remains is pure implementation: <code>fn call(&amp;self, args) -&gt; Result&amp;lt;Value, EvalError&gt;</code> bodies. No trait boilerplate, no bridge code, no per-lib plugin scaffolding.</p><p><strong>Done when:</strong> all native libs are migrated per ADR-0083 (FFI bindings, not Rust bodies); their Rust crates deleted entirely.</p><h3 id="phase-5-—-capability-declaration-in-handl" tabindex="-1">Phase 5 — Capability declaration in handl <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-5-—-capability-declaration-in-handl" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 5 — Capability declaration in handl”">​</a></h3><p>Move <code>CapabilityDecl</code> from Rust <code>Language</code> construction into <code>.hnl</code> source, so a handl library declares its own capability surface.</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>-- fs.hnl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>cap &quot;fs.read&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>cap &quot;fs.write&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>op &quot;fs.read&quot; : Path -&gt; Bytes</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ...</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This requires:</p><ul><li><strong>Surface syntax</strong> for <code>cap</code> declarations in <code>.hnl</code>.</li><li><strong>Loader integration</strong> — the module loader extracts <code>cap</code> declarations and feeds them into the <code>Language</code>&#39;s capability set (currently built in Rust via <code>CapabilityDecl::new</code>).</li><li><strong><code>HandlLibraryPlugin</code> integration</strong> — the plugin reads the declared capabilities and contributes them via <code>ContributionContext</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Done when:</strong> a handl library&#39;s capability set is fully determined by its <code>.hnl</code> source; no Rust <code>CapabilityDecl::new</code> calls remain for migrated libs.</p><h3 id="phase-6-—-adr-0069-phase-7-arrows-as-importable-modules" tabindex="-1">Phase 6 — ADR-0069 Phase 7: arrows/ as importable modules <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-6-—-adr-0069-phase-7-arrows-as-importable-modules" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 6 — ADR-0069 Phase 7: arrows/ as importable modules”">​</a></h3><p>Move <code>handl-packages/</code> arrow files to be importable <code>.hnl</code> modules (ADR-0069 Phase 7). The loader already supports this; the eval wiring from Phase 1 makes the imported arrows callable. Verify the full cascade: atoms → insert → sort → user programs, all via imports with compositional inference, under each of the three normalization strategies.</p><p><strong>Done when:</strong> <code>handl-packages/**/*.hnl</code> are importable; a user program can <code>import sort from &quot;collection/sort&quot;</code> and call it; inference composes across the import boundary.</p><h3 id="multi-target-support-—-interface-impl-split" tabindex="-1">Multi-target support — interface/impl split <a class="header-anchor" href="#multi-target-support-—-interface-impl-split" aria-label="Permalink to “Multi-target support — interface/impl split”">​</a></h3><p>The stdlib is already platform-agnostic by design (ADR-0002). Native libs follow an <strong>interface/impl</strong> split:</p><ul><li><strong>Interface crate</strong> (<code>plugin-lib-X</code>) — abstract trait, no I/O deps, platform-neutral. Defines the API surface.</li><li><strong>Impl crate</strong> (<code>plugin-lib-X-std</code> / <code>-native</code> / <code>-wasm</code>) — concrete backend for a specific target. The host picks which impl to register.</li></ul><p>Existing examples:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>plugin-lib-os          → abstract traits (Fs, Env, Path, Time, Cmd, …)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>plugin-lib-os-std      → impl via Rust stdlib (std::fs, std::process)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>plugin-lib-http-client → abstract trait (HttpClient)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>plugin-lib-http-client-std → impl via ureq</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>plugin-lib-ws-whatwg   → abstract</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>plugin-lib-ws-whatwg-native → impl via native syscalls</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>plugin-lib-ws-whatwg-wasm   → impl via WASM (cfg target_arch = &quot;wasm32&quot;)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The <code>op</code>/<code>handl</code> migration preserves and strengthens this split:</p><ul><li><strong>Interface</strong> → <code>.hnl</code> file with <code>op</code> declarations + axioms + <code>cap</code> declarations (platform-neutral, shared across all targets)</li><li><strong>Impl</strong> → Rust <code>handl</code> bodies registered under the <code>op</code> names (one per target: <code>std</code>, <code>native</code>, <code>wasm</code>, <code>js</code>, …)</li></ul><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>-- os.hnl (platform-neutral, shared across all targets)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>--- fs.read — read a file as bytes.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>--- Contract: IsUtf8(output)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>op &quot;fs.read&quot; : Path -&gt; Bytes</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  guarantee: IsUtf8(output)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>cap &quot;fs.read&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="language-rust"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">rust</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;">//</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#BBBBBB;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> os-std crate (Rust native)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">handl </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">fs.read</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#0F0;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> =</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> rust_std_fs_read   </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;">//</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#BBBBBB;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> std::fs::read</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;">//</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#BBBBBB;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> os-wasm crate (WASM target)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">handl </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">fs.read</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#0F0;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> =</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> wasm_fs_read        </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;">//</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#BBBBBB;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> browser fetch API</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;">//</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#BBBBBB;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> os-js crate (JS target, future)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">handl </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">fs.read</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#0F0;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> =</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> js_fs_read          </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;">//</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#BBBBBB;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> Node.js fs.readFile</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The host selects the impl crate based on target. The <code>.hnl</code> interface, axioms, proofs, capabilities, and doc comments are shared across all targets — only the <code>handl</code> bindings change.</p><p><strong>Runtime support:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Interpreted (tree-walk):</strong> works today — <code>op</code> dispatches to <code>ModuleLookup</code>.</li><li><strong>JIT (cranelift):</strong> <code>op</code> lowers to <code>ArrSource::Foreign</code> in Scheme IR — same dispatch path, different eval engine.</li><li><strong>WASM:</strong> <code>ws-whatwg-wasm</code> already proves the pattern via <code>cfg(target_arch = &quot;wasm32&quot;)</code>.</li><li><strong>JS:</strong> a future <code>plugin-lib-X-js</code> impl crate would register JS-backed handls under the same <code>op</code> names.</li><li><strong>Sandbox:</strong> the <code>cap</code> declarations + <code>requires</code>/<code>guarantee</code> enforcement (Phase 3) form the sandbox boundary — a sandboxed host refuses to register handls for caps it doesn&#39;t allow.</li></ul><p>The <code>op</code>/<code>handl</code> split <em>is</em> the target abstraction. The interface (<code>.hnl</code>) is target-neutral; the impl (Rust handls) is per-target. No additional mechanism is needed for multi-target support — it is inherent in the design.</p><h2 id="appendix-full-stdlib-inventory-and-migration-map" tabindex="-1">Appendix: Full stdlib inventory and migration map <a class="header-anchor" href="#appendix-full-stdlib-inventory-and-migration-map" aria-label="Permalink to “Appendix: Full stdlib inventory and migration map”">​</a></h2><p>Every <code>plugin-lib-*</code> crate, its category, approximate Rust line count, and migration path. Categories:</p><ul><li><strong>Pure</strong> — logic is (or can be) handl-authored; no Rust crate deps. Migrates via <code>HandlModule</code> (Phase 1).</li><li><strong>Mixed</strong> — some functions are pure handl, others wrap Rust crates. Migrates in two steps: pure functions via <code>HandlModule</code> (Phase 1), native functions via <code>op</code>/<code>handl</code> (Phase 4).</li><li><strong>Native</strong> — all functions wrap Rust crates or syscalls. Migrates via <code>op</code>/<code>handl</code> FFI (Phase 4).</li><li><strong>Interface</strong> — defines the API surface (module/function names), delegates to a <code>-std</code> or native variant. Migrates to a <code>.hnl</code> file with <code>op</code> declarations (Phase 4); the delegate crate provides the handls.</li><li><strong>Special</strong> — engine crates, the loader itself. Stays Rust.</li></ul><h3 id="already-migrated" tabindex="-1">Already migrated <a class="header-anchor" href="#already-migrated" aria-label="Permalink to “Already migrated”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Crate</th><th>Lines</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-stats</code></td><td>390</td><td>Migrated to <code>HandlModule</code> (proof-of-concept)</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-handl</code></td><td>637</td><td>The generic loader itself — stays Rust</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="pure-logic-phase-1" tabindex="-1">Pure-logic (Phase 1) <a class="header-anchor" href="#pure-logic-phase-1" aria-label="Permalink to “Pure-logic (Phase 1)”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Crate</th><th>Lines</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-hello</code></td><td>329</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>, uses <code>include_str!</code> — simplest migration</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-dedent</code></td><td>268</td><td>String manipulation, no native deps</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-type</code></td><td>195</td><td>Type introspection helpers, no native deps</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-compress</code></td><td>100</td><td>Interface to compress-gzip</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-kv</code></td><td>305</td><td>Interface to kv-hashmap</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-kv-hashmap</code></td><td>186</td><td>In-memory key-value store, pure logic</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-encoding</code></td><td>97</td><td>Interface to base64/hex</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-archive</code></td><td>110</td><td>Interface to archive-tar</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="mixed-—-pure-logic-native-ops-phase-1-for-pure-parts-phase-4-for-native-parts" tabindex="-1">Mixed — pure logic + native ops (Phase 1 for pure parts, Phase 4 for native parts) <a class="header-anchor" href="#mixed-—-pure-logic-native-ops-phase-1-for-pure-parts-phase-4-for-native-parts" aria-label="Permalink to “Mixed — pure logic + native ops (Phase 1 for pure parts, Phase 4 for native parts)”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Crate</th><th>Lines</th><th>Native deps</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-collections</code></td><td>4446</td><td>Rust <code>Vec</code>/<code>HashMap</code></td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; array/list/deque/stack/queue/map/set — some pure, some wrap Rust</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-utils</code></td><td>10165</td><td>Rust <code>Math</code>, string ops</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; fn/math/str/obj/type/cmp/bool/option/result/set/mood/parse/serialize — mixed</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-cmp</code></td><td>893</td><td>Rust comparison</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; comparison logic — mostly pure, some native</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-os</code></td><td>3011</td><td>Interface only</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; delegates to <code>os-std</code> — interface crate</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-http-client</code></td><td>853</td><td>Interface only</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; delegates to <code>http-client-std</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-http-server</code></td><td>819</td><td><code>hyper</code></td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; server logic + native HTTP</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-json</code></td><td>180</td><td><code>serde_json</code></td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; parse/stringify logic + native serde</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-toml</code></td><td>177</td><td><code>toml</code> crate</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; parse/stringify + native</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-yaml</code></td><td>179</td><td><code>serde_yaml</code></td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; parse/stringify + native</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-crypto</code></td><td>261</td><td><code>sha2</code>/<code>aes</code></td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; hash/encrypt ops</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-datetime</code></td><td>1228</td><td><code>chrono</code></td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; date/time logic + native chrono</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-rand</code></td><td>3182</td><td><code>rand</code> crate</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; random generation</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-unicode</code></td><td>459</td><td><code>unicode</code> libs</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; unicode ops</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-intl</code></td><td>4957</td><td><code>icu</code></td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; internationalization</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-otlp</code></td><td>984</td><td><code>otlp</code></td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; telemetry</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-test</code></td><td>1238</td><td>native</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; test framework</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-kv</code></td><td>305</td><td>interface</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; delegates to kv-hashmap</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-ws-rfc-memory</code></td><td>602</td><td>in-memory</td><td>Has <code>.hnl</code>; pure websocket frame logic</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="native-—-all-rust-phase-4" tabindex="-1">Native — all Rust (Phase 4) <a class="header-anchor" href="#native-—-all-rust-phase-4" aria-label="Permalink to “Native — all Rust (Phase 4)”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Crate</th><th>Lines</th><th>Native deps</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-base64</code></td><td>126</td><td><code>base64</code></td><td>Encode/decode</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-hex</code></td><td>123</td><td><code>hex</code></td><td>Encode/decode</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-ip</code></td><td>1326</td><td><code>ipnet</code></td><td>IP/CIDR/socket</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-parse</code></td><td>84</td><td>native</td><td>Parser utilities</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-prim</code></td><td>383</td><td>native</td><td>Primitive ops</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-serialize</code></td><td>151</td><td><code>serde</code></td><td>Serialization</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-tag</code></td><td>1026</td><td>native</td><td>Tagging system</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-tls</code></td><td>762</td><td><code>rustls</code></td><td>TLS interface</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-tls-native</code></td><td>533</td><td><code>rustls</code></td><td>TLS implementation</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-url-rfc</code></td><td>216</td><td><code>url</code></td><td>RFC URL parsing</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-url-whatwg</code></td><td>217</td><td><code>url</code></td><td>WHATWG URL parsing</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-archive-tar</code></td><td>281</td><td><code>tar</code></td><td>Tar archive</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-compress-gzip</code></td><td>224</td><td><code>flate2</code></td><td>Gzip compression</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-arrow-iter</code></td><td>204</td><td>native</td><td>Arrow iteration</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-concurrent</code></td><td>6622</td><td><code>tokio</code></td><td>Concurrency</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-os-std</code></td><td>936</td><td><code>std::fs</code>/<code>process</code></td><td>OS operations</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-http-client-std</code></td><td>138</td><td><code>reqwest</code></td><td>HTTP client impl</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-http-server-std</code></td><td>491</td><td><code>hyper</code></td><td>HTTP server impl</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-otlp-std</code></td><td>468</td><td><code>otlp</code></td><td>OTLP implementation</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-ws-rfc</code></td><td>2407</td><td>native</td><td>WebSocket RFC</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-ws-rfc-native</code></td><td>1505</td><td>native</td><td>WebSocket native</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-ws-whatwg-native</code></td><td>423</td><td>native</td><td>WebSocket WHATWG</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-ws-whatwg-wasm</code></td><td>486</td><td>native</td><td>WebSocket WASM</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="special-—-stays-rust-no-migration" tabindex="-1">Special — stays Rust (no migration) <a class="header-anchor" href="#special-—-stays-rust-no-migration" aria-label="Permalink to “Special — stays Rust (no migration)”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Crate</th><th>Lines</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-lang-compile</code></td><td>554</td><td>Language engine — compile</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-lang-eval</code></td><td>514</td><td>Language engine — eval</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-lang-lex</code></td><td>513</td><td>Language engine — lex</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-lang-parse</code></td><td>488</td><td>Language engine — parse</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-lang-run-compiled</code></td><td>493</td><td>Language engine — run compiled</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-lang-typecheck</code></td><td>534</td><td>Language engine — typecheck</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-lib-handl</code></td><td>637</td><td>The generic loader itself</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="migration-summary" tabindex="-1">Migration summary <a class="header-anchor" href="#migration-summary" aria-label="Permalink to “Migration summary”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Crates</th><th>Total Rust lines</th><th>Migration phase</th><th>Rust after migration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Already migrated</td><td>2</td><td>1027</td><td>Done</td><td>~637 (loader only)</td></tr><tr><td>Pure-logic</td><td>8</td><td>~1590</td><td>Phase 1</td><td>0 (all <code>.hnl</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Mixed</td><td>18</td><td>~23900</td><td>Phase 1 + 4</td><td><code>op</code> handls only</td></tr><tr><td>Native</td><td>23</td><td>~17600</td><td>Phase 4</td><td><code>op</code> handls only</td></tr><tr><td>Special</td><td>7</td><td>~3733</td><td>Never</td><td>stays Rust</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>58</strong></td><td><strong>~47850</strong></td><td></td><td><strong>~3733 + handl bodies</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>After migration, ~44,000 lines of Rust scaffolding (Module/Plugin/Function trait impls, bridge code, per-lib runtime construction) is deleted. What remains is ~3,733 lines of engine/loader code + the native <code>Function</code> bodies (the handls) + N <code>.hnl</code> files carrying all logic, contracts, proofs, capabilities, and documentation.</p>`,140)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};